// The Bit allocator (runtime/alloc/alloc.bit, #1362) — the Stage-2 port of
// the deleted native runtime's `alloc` module, exercised as a real linked binary.
//
// The module under test is imported from `../../../runtime/alloc`, so this
// compiles the SAME source the runtime will ship rather than a copy.
//
// WHY THIS TEST CAN RUN ON EVERY TARGET. `runtime/alloc/alloc.bit` is deliberately
// platform-free: the page seam is inverted so the caller supplies mapped pages
// (see the module header — `syscall` is Linux-only and `extern function` is
// Darwin-only, and both reject even an uncalled declaration, so no single
// source form can carry both). That inversion is what lets one test exercise
// the real allocator on aarch64-macos, aarch64-linux and x86_64-linux without a
// line of per-target code: this file plays the role of the OS, handing out
// page-aligned chunks from a bump arena instead of from mmap. Every byte of
// allocator POLICY — classification, alignment, the free-list shape, the carve
// order, the counters — is the shipped code.
//
// The real page providers (`runtime/alloc/linux`, `runtime/alloc/darwin`) are
// exercised separately, on real kernel memory, by `../alloclinux` and
// `../allocdarwin` — they cannot be reached from here because a `syscall` and
// an `extern function` cannot coexist in one module.
//
// Ten properties, all deterministic:
//
//   (a) SIZE-CLASS TABLE — the 37 classes are dumped as a position-weighted
//       checksum, so a single wrong or transposed entry changes the output.
//       `runtime/alloc.bit` computes the table arithmetically where the
//       deleted native runtime's `alloc` module spelled it as a literal array
//       (Bit cannot index a table under `@nosplit`), which makes this
//       checksum the one check against that class of typo.
//
//   (b) TABLE INVARIANTS — the classes strictly increase, and every power of
//       two from 8 to 16384 IS a class. That second one is the invariant the
//       deleted native runtime's comment rests its alignment guarantee on
//       ("any supported alignment is itself a class, so a satisfying slot
//       always exists"), so it is checked rather than assumed.
//
//   (c) CLASSIFY CONTRACT — over a matrix of sizes x alignments, the class
//       `allocClassify` picks must satisfy `cs >= alignUp(size, alignment)` and
//       `cs % alignment == 0`, and must be the SMALLEST class that does. That
//       is the full specification of the function, checked without a golden:
//       a classify that returned a merely-adequate class rather than the best
//       one still passes the first two conditions and fails this.
//
//   (d) ALIGNMENT — every address handed out is aligned to the requested
//       alignment, across every power of two from 8 to 4096 and sizes that
//       straddle class boundaries. This is the property the GC depends on.
//
//   (e) REUSE — freeing a slot and allocating the same class again returns the
//       IDENTICAL address (the deleted native runtime's `alloc` module's own
//       unit test asserts exactly this), which proves the intrusive free list
//       recycles rather than re-carving.
//
//   (f) CARVE — one span of a given class yields exactly `spanBytes / classSize`
//       distinct slots, all inside the span and all class-size apart. This is
//       the metadata shape the collector's sweep walks back onto the list.
//
//   (g) COUNTERS — `liveBytes` tracks outstanding bytes exactly and returns to
//       0 once everything is freed; `mappedBytes` equals the spans actually
//       taken. The deleted native runtime's leak metric.
//
//   (i) DIRECT-MAPPED (LARGE) PATH — requests above allocMaxSmall bypass the
//       size classes: mapped straight from the OS, rounded up to a page, and
//       accounted separately. Nothing else in this file reaches that path, and
//       the differential covers only the `allocLargeLen` arithmetic and not its
//       composition with the counters, so the deleted native runtime's own
//       "large direct-mapped allocations are page aligned and unmapped on
//       free" unit test is mirrored here: page alignment, exact live/mapped
//       accounting in BOTH directions, and the full rounded length being
//       writable memory.
//
//   (j)+(k) PER-SPAN OCCUPANCY (#2374) and SPAN RECLAMATION (#2375) — sibling
//       file `./spanaccounting.bit` (SPEC §17.1), split out to keep this file
//       under the 800-line gate (E0200); `main()` below just calls `occRun(a)`
//       and `recRun(a)`. Their full description lives in that file's header.
//
//   (h) CHURN WITH SENTINELS — a ring of live allocations is recycled 20000
//       times; each allocation carries a unique tag in its first word which
//       must still be there at free time. If the allocator ever handed the same
//       slot out twice, one tag would overwrite the other and the count of
//       intact sentinels would drop. This is the deleted native runtime's own
//       stress test ported, and the first word is exactly where the intrusive
//       next-pointer lives, so it is also the byte the free list is most
//       likely to corrupt.
//
// Under BIT_GC=stress the collector fires at every safepoint. The whole arena
// is a live `[]i64` holding raw addresses; it must stay rooted across every
// collection while only untraced `int` addresses reach the allocator, so a
// rooting or pointer-map bug shows up as a wrong answer rather than a crash.
//
// SINGLE-THREADED BY DESIGN. Mutual exclusion is the lock's property, not the
// allocator's, and `_tests_/stress/spinlock` already proves it under contention.
// Holding the lock across this file's non-`@nosplit` arena helper could reach a
// safepoint, and a `@nosplit` spinner cannot yield to release it (the deadlock
// hazard documented in runtime/spinlock.bit), so this test takes the lock on a
// single thread — which still exercises the real acquire/release path around
// every allocation.

import {
  allocNumClasses, allocMaxSmall, allocSpanBytes, heapWords,
  allocClassSize, allocAlignUp, allocClassify, allocLargeLen,
  allocSpanHdrWords, allocSpanOf, allocSpanSlots, allocSpanKeep, allocSlotIndex,
  allocSpanClass, allocSpanTotal, allocSpanFree, allocSpanNext, allocSpanOwned,
  heapInit, heapLiveBytes, heapMappedBytes,
  heapTakeSmallLocked, heapGiveSmallLocked, heapRefillLocked,
  heapReclaimSpansLocked, heapClassSpans, heapClassEmpty,
  heapLargeAllocLocked, heapLargeFreeLocked,
  heapCheckSpansLocked, heapSpanViolations, heapSpansReleasedLocked,
  heapSpanCachePushLocked, heapSpanCacheTakeLocked,
} from "../../../runtime/alloc"
import { spinAcquire, spinRelease, spinUnlocked } from "../../../runtime"

const PAGE = 4096
// One span is consumed per size class on that class's first allocation (the
// deleted native runtime behaved identically), and property (e) touches all
// 37 classes, so the arena must cover every class plus the carve span,
// property (j)'s three and the large path.
const MAX_SPANS = 56
const SPAN_WORDS = 16384 // allocSpanBytes / 8
// MAX_SPANS * SPAN_WORDS + 2048 alignment slack + 262144 words (2MB) for the
// direct-mapped requests in property (i), which bypass spans entirely.
const ARENA_WORDS = 1181696
const RING = 128
const CHURN = 20000

// Property (j). Class 28 is 4096-byte slots, so a span yields 31 of them once
// the header is carved out: three spans is 93 slots — enough for a scrambled
// order to interleave the spans repeatedly, small enough to re-check every
// span's count after every single free.
const OCC_CLASS = 28
const OCC_SPANS = 3

// Property (k). Four spans of the same class: one more than `allocSpanKeep`, so
// the reclaim trigger has to fire, and enough that the pass has to splice spans
// out of the MIDDLE of the class's span list rather than only off its ends.
const REC_SPANS = 4

// Properties (j) #2374 and (k) #2375 are `./spanaccounting.bit`. Property (l),
// #3045, is `./spancache.bit` and property (m), #3100, is `./classify.bit` —
// sibling files, same module (SPEC §17.1), split out to keep this file under
// the 800-line gate (E0200). Their own consts and helpers live there; `main()`
// below just calls `occRun(a)`, `recRun(a)`, `spancacheRun()` and
// `classifyRun()`.
//
//   (m) CLASSIFY FAST PATH ≡ LINEAR SCAN. #3100 replaced the size-class scan
//       with a closed-form shortcut for classes 0-3. Property (c) above checks
//       `allocClassify` against its SPECIFICATION over a sample; (m) checks the
//       shipped function against the ORIGINAL SCAN, kept verbatim, over EVERY
//       (size, alignment) pair in the domain — because a shortcut wrong for one
//       size in 16384 passes a sample and corrupts the heap.

// Arena state words.
const A_NEXT = 0
const A_LIMIT = 1
const A_SPANS = 2

// The `*i64` addressing the 8-aligned word at `addr`. The test's own copy of
// the allocator's private helper — the allocator fabricates pointers the same
// way (§11.4: `*i64` arithmetic scales by 8).
fn wordAt(addr: int): *i64 {
  let z: *i64 = nil
  return z + addr / 8
}

// Hand out `len` bytes aligned to `align` from the bump arena. This is the test
// standing in for the OS: it is exactly the contract `mmap` fulfils for the
// deleted native runtime (page-aligned, never reused until the process exits).
// Returns 0 when exhausted, as `mmap` returning null does.
fn arenaTake(a: []i64, len: int, align: int): int {
  let addr = allocAlignUp(a[A_NEXT], align)
  if (addr + len > a[A_LIMIT]) {
    return 0
  }
  a[A_NEXT] = addr + len
  return addr
}

// Take one span, counting it. Bounded by MAX_SPANS.
fn spanTake(a: []i64): int {
  if (a[A_SPANS] >= MAX_SPANS) {
    return 0
  }
  let s = arenaTake(a, allocSpanBytes, allocSpanBytes)
  if (s == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  a[A_SPANS] = a[A_SPANS] + 1
  return s
}

// The composed allocator entry point: the glue that in the shipped runtime is
// `runtime/alloc/{linux,darwin}`'s `mapPages` in place of `arenaTake`. The lock
// is held across the whole operation INCLUDING the page take, exactly as the
// deleted native runtime held it across its refill mmap (`acquire();
// defer release();` spans all of `Heap.alloc`).
fn heapAlloc(h: *i64, lk: *i32, a: []i64, size: int, alignment: int): int {
  spinAcquire(lk)
  let addr = 0
  let idx = allocClassify(size, alignment)
  if (idx < 0) {
    let len = allocLargeLen(size, PAGE)
    let m = arenaTake(a, len, PAGE)
    if (m != 0) {
      heapLargeAllocLocked(h, size, len)
      addr = m
    }
  } else {
    addr = heapTakeSmallLocked(h, idx, size)
    if (addr == 0) {
      let span = spanTake(a)
      if (span != 0) {
        // `owned` is FALSE for every span this glue carves, and that is the
        // truthful answer: the arena is a Bit-managed `[]i64`, not pages this
        // file mapped, so nothing here may ever be unmapped (#2375). Property
        // (k) is the one that says `true` — and says why.
        heapRefillLocked(h, idx, span, false)
        addr = heapTakeSmallLocked(h, idx, size)
      }
    }
  }
  spinRelease(lk)
  return addr
}

// The mirror of `heapAlloc`. `size`/`alignment` must match the original request
// — the deleted native runtime's free contract, unchanged.
fn heapFree(h: *i64, lk: *i32, addr: int, size: int, alignment: int) {
  spinAcquire(lk)
  let idx = allocClassify(size, alignment)
  if (idx < 0) {
    heapLargeFreeLocked(h, size, allocLargeLen(size, PAGE))
  } else {
    // The result is the allocator asking for a reclaim pass. Ignored here on
    // purpose: these spans are not releasable, so acting on it would be the one
    // thing #2375 must never do. Property (k) drives the pass deliberately.
    let _ = heapGiveSmallLocked(h, addr, idx, size)
  }
  spinRelease(lk)
}

class TableSums {
  sum: int,
  weighted: int,
}

class CarveResult {
  ok: int,
  got: int,
}

class ChurnResult {
  ok: int,
  intact: int,
  checked: int,
}

class SlotCheck {
  intact: int,
  checked: int,
}

// The churn ring: one entry per slot, split across three parallel slices
// exactly as it was when it lived in `main`. It is a class only so that
// `drainSlot` can take the whole ring as one parameter.
class Ring {
  addr: []i64,
  size: []i64,
  tag: []i64,
}

// The heap's byte counters before a property runs, so the property can assert
// it gave every byte back.
class HeapBase {
  live: int,
  mapped: int,
}

// ---- (a) size-class table: a position-weighted checksum ---------------------
// Weighting by (i+1) makes the checksum sensitive to ORDER, so two
// transposed classes change it; a plain sum would not.
fn tableChecksum(): TableSums {
  let tsum = 0
  let twsum = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < allocNumClasses) {
    let cs = allocClassSize(i)
    tsum = tsum + cs
    twsum = twsum + cs * (i + 1)
    i = i + 1
  }
  return TableSums{ sum: tsum, weighted: twsum }
}

// ---- (b) table invariants ---------------------------------------------------
// Strictly increasing, first is 8, last is allocMaxSmall.
fn tableInvariants(): int {
  let mono = 1
  let j = 1
  while (j < allocNumClasses) {
    if (allocClassSize(j) <= allocClassSize(j - 1)) {
      mono = 0
    }
    j = j + 1
  }
  if (allocClassSize(0) != 8) {
    mono = 0
  }
  if (allocClassSize(allocNumClasses - 1) != allocMaxSmall) {
    mono = 0
  }
  return mono
}

// Every power of two in [8, allocMaxSmall] is itself a class — the invariant
// the alignment guarantee rests on.
fn powersAreClasses(): int {
  let powsOk = 1
  let p = 8
  while (p <= allocMaxSmall) {
    let found = 0
    let k = 0
    while (k < allocNumClasses) {
      if (allocClassSize(k) == p) {
        found = 1
      }
      k = k + 1
    }
    if (found == 0) {
      powsOk = 0
    }
    p = p * 2
  }
  return powsOk
}

// Is there ANY class that fits `needed` bytes at alignment `al`? Only when
// there is none may `allocClassify` refuse the request.
fn anyClassFits(needed: int, al: int): bool {
  let k = 0
  let any = false
  while (k < allocNumClasses) {
    let cs = allocClassSize(k)
    if (cs >= needed && cs % al == 0) {
      any = true
    }
    k = k + 1
  }
  return any
}

// No class BELOW `idx` may also satisfy the request — the "smallest that fits"
// half of the contract.
fn noSmallerFits(idx: int, needed: int, al: int): bool {
  let k = 0
  let none = true
  while (k < idx) {
    let cs = allocClassSize(k)
    if (cs >= needed && cs % al == 0) {
      none = false
    }
    k = k + 1
  }
  return none
}

fn classifyOneOk(sz: int, al: int): bool {
  let idx = allocClassify(sz, al)
  let needed = allocAlignUp(sz, al)
  if (idx < 0) {
    // Only legitimate when no class can satisfy it at all.
    return !anyClassFits(needed, al)
  }
  let cs = allocClassSize(idx)
  return cs >= needed && cs % al == 0 && noSmallerFits(idx, needed, al)
}

// ---- (c) classify contract: smallest class that fits AND divides ------------
fn classifyContract(): int {
  let classifyOk = 1
  let alignIdx = 0
  while (alignIdx < 10) {
    let al = 8 << alignIdx // 8 .. 4096
    let sz = 1
    while (sz <= allocMaxSmall) {
      if (!classifyOneOk(sz, al)) {
        classifyOk = 0
      }
      sz = sz * 3 + 1 // straddles class boundaries
    }
    alignIdx = alignIdx + 1
  }
  return classifyOk
}

// The six request sizes property (d) drives at every alignment.
fn alignSize(si: int): int {
  if (si == 1) {
    return 7
  }
  if (si == 2) {
    return 8
  }
  if (si == 3) {
    return 17
  }
  if (si == 4) {
    return 100
  }
  if (si == 5) {
    return 1000
  }
  return 1
}

// ---- (d) alignment across powers of two -------------------------------------
// `a` is main's local and only its header travels here, so the arena's backing
// store stays in main's frame and stays rooted for the whole run.
fn alignProps(h: *i64, lk: *i32, a: []i64): int {
  let alignOk = 1
  let ai = 0
  while (ai < 10) {
    let al = 8 << ai // 8 .. 4096
    let si = 0
    while (si < 6) {
      let sz = alignSize(si)
      let addr = heapAlloc(h, lk, a, sz, al)
      if (addr == 0) {
        alignOk = 0 // arena exhausted: never deref
      } else {
        if (addr % al != 0) {
          alignOk = 0
        }
        heapFree(h, lk, addr, sz, al)
      }
      si = si + 1
    }
    ai = ai + 1
  }
  return alignOk
}

// ---- (e) reuse: free then re-alloc returns the same address -----------------
fn reuseProps(h: *i64, lk: *i32, a: []i64): int {
  let reuseOk = 1
  let ri = 0
  while (ri < allocNumClasses) {
    let cs = allocClassSize(ri)
    let p1 = heapAlloc(h, lk, a, cs, 8)
    if (p1 == 0) {
      reuseOk = 0 // arena exhausted
    } else {
      if (heapLiveBytes(h) != cs) {
        reuseOk = 0
      }
      heapFree(h, lk, p1, cs, 8)
      if (heapLiveBytes(h) != 0) {
        reuseOk = 0
      }
      let p2 = heapAlloc(h, lk, a, cs, 8)
      if (p2 != p1) {
        reuseOk = 0
      } // recycled, not re-carved
      heapFree(h, lk, p2, cs, 8)
    }
    ri = ri + 1
  }
  return reuseOk
}

// One popped slot: inside the span, clear of the header at its base, and
// exactly one class size below the previous one.
fn carveSlotOk(s: int, span: int, cs4: int, got: int, prev: int): bool {
  if (s < span + allocSpanHdrWords * 8) {
    return false
  }
  if (s + cs4 > span + allocSpanBytes) {
    return false
  }
  return got == 0 || prev - s == cs4 // descending, class-size apart
}

// ---- (f) carve: one span yields exactly spanBytes/classSize distinct slots --
// A fresh heap and a fresh span, so the count is unambiguous. `h2buf`/`l2buf`
// are locals of THIS frame for the whole life of the second heap: nothing
// outside this property ever touches it.
fn carveProps(a: []i64): CarveResult {
  let h2buf = []i64(heapWords)
  let h2 = ptrOf(h2buf)
  let l2buf = []i32(1)
  let lk2 = ptrOf(l2buf)
  atomicStore(lk2, spinUnlocked)
  heapInit(h2)
  let carveOk = 1
  let carveClass = 20 // classSize 1024 -> 128 slots
  let cs4 = allocClassSize(carveClass)
  let span = spanTake(a)
  if (span == 0) {
    carveOk = 0
  }
  spinAcquire(lk2)
  heapRefillLocked(h2, carveClass, span, false)
  spinRelease(lk2)
  if (heapMappedBytes(h2) != allocSpanBytes) {
    carveOk = 0
  }
  // Not `allocSpanBytes / cs4`: the span header at the base costs the class its
  // first `ceil(hdrBytes / cs4)` slots (#2374).
  let want = allocSpanSlots(carveClass)
  let got = 0
  let prev = 0
  let drained = 0
  // Statically bounded: a correct list yields exactly `want` slots then 0, so
  // `want + 1` pops always suffice. The bound is not decoration — an allocator
  // that fails to unlink the slot it hands out re-serves the same address
  // forever, and without it this loop spins instead of failing (found by
  // mutation M5).
  let guard = 0
  while (drained == 0 && guard <= want) {
    spinAcquire(lk2)
    let s = heapTakeSmallLocked(h2, carveClass, cs4)
    spinRelease(lk2)
    if (s == 0) {
      drained = 1
    } else {
      if (!carveSlotOk(s, span, cs4, got, prev)) {
        carveOk = 0
      }
      prev = s
      got = got + 1
    }
    guard = guard + 1
  }
  if (drained == 0) {
    carveOk = 0
  } // list never terminated
  if (got != want) {
    carveOk = 0
  }
  return CarveResult{ ok: carveOk, got: got }
}

// The three request sizes property (i) drives, all above allocMaxSmall.
fn largeSize(li: int): int {
  if (li == 1) {
    return 100000
  }
  if (li == 2) {
    return 1048576
  }
  return allocMaxSmall + 1
}

// One direct-mapped request: page aligned, both counters moved by exactly the
// documented amounts, the whole rounded-up length writable, and every byte of
// it given back on free.
fn largeOneOk(h: *i64, lk: *i32, a: []i64, lsz: int, base: HeapBase): bool {
  if (allocClassify(lsz, 16) >= 0) {
    return false // must NOT be a size class
  }
  let want = allocLargeLen(lsz, PAGE)
  let addr = heapAlloc(h, lk, a, lsz, 16)
  if (addr == 0) {
    return false
  }
  // direct maps are page aligned
  let takenOk = addr % PAGE == 0 &&
    heapLiveBytes(h) == base.live + lsz &&
    heapMappedBytes(h) == base.mapped + want
  // The whole rounded-up length is real, writable memory.
  let w = wordAt(addr)
  *w = 424242
  let wEnd = wordAt(addr + want - 8)
  *wEnd = 515151
  let back = *w
  heapFree(h, lk, addr, lsz, 16)
  // mapping returned
  return takenOk &&
    back == 424242 &&
    heapLiveBytes(h) == base.live &&
    heapMappedBytes(h) == base.mapped
}

// ---- (i) the direct-mapped (large) path -------------------------------------
// Requests above allocMaxSmall bypass the size classes entirely: they are
// mapped straight from the OS, rounded up to a page, and accounted through
// `heapLargeAllocLocked`/`heapLargeFreeLocked`. Nothing above reaches that
// path — every size so far is <= allocMaxSmall and so classifies as SMALL —
// and the deleted native runtime had a dedicated unit test for it ("large
// direct-mapped allocations are page aligned and unmapped on free"), so it
// is mirrored here rather than left to the differential, which only covers
// the `allocLargeLen` arithmetic and not its composition with the counters.
fn largeProps(h: *i64, lk: *i32, a: []i64): int {
  let base = HeapBase{ live: heapLiveBytes(h), mapped: heapMappedBytes(h) }
  let largeOk = 1
  let li = 0
  while (li < 3) {
    if (!largeOneOk(h, lk, a, largeSize(li), base)) {
      largeOk = 0
    }
    li = li + 1
  }
  return largeOk
}

// Check one ring slot's sentinel and give the block back. An empty slot is
// neither checked nor intact, which is what the churn loop's first `RING`
// rounds see.
fn drainSlot(h: *i64, lk: *i32, r: Ring, slot: int): SlotCheck {
  let old = r.addr[slot]
  if (old == 0) {
    return SlotCheck{ intact: 0, checked: 0 }
  }
  let w = wordAt(old)
  let seen = *w
  let intact = 0
  if (seen == r.tag[slot]) {
    intact = 1
  }
  heapFree(h, lk, old, r.size[slot], 8)
  r.addr[slot] = 0
  return SlotCheck{ intact: intact, checked: 1 }
}

// ---- (g)+(h) churn with sentinels -------------------------------------------
fn churnProps(h: *i64, lk: *i32, a: []i64): ChurnResult {
  let r = Ring{ addr: []i64(RING), size: []i64(RING), tag: []i64(RING) }
  let intact = 0
  let checked = 0
  let churnOk = 1
  let n = 0
  while (n < CHURN) {
    let slot = n % RING
    let c = drainSlot(h, lk, r, slot)
    intact = intact + c.intact
    checked = checked + c.checked
    // Deterministic sizes spanning many classes, staying inside the arena.
    let sz = 1 + n * 37 % 3000
    let addr = heapAlloc(h, lk, a, sz, 8)
    if (addr == 0) {
      churnOk = 0 // arena exhausted: never deref
    } else {
      if (addr % 8 != 0) {
        churnOk = 0
      }
      let tag = n * 2654435761 + 1
      let w2 = wordAt(addr)
      *w2 = tag // sentinel in the first word
      r.addr[slot] = addr
      r.size[slot] = sz
      r.tag[slot] = tag
    }
    n = n + 1
  }
  // Drain survivors.
  let d = 0
  while (d < RING) {
    let c = drainSlot(h, lk, r, d)
    intact = intact + c.intact
    checked = checked + c.checked
    d = d + 1
  }
  return ChurnResult{ ok: churnOk, intact: intact, checked: checked }
}

fn main() {
  let tbl = tableChecksum()
  let mono = tableInvariants()
  let powsOk = powersAreClasses()
  let classifyOk = classifyContract()

  // ---- heap + arena ---------------------------------------------------------
  // `hbuf`, `lbuf` and `arena` are locals of THIS frame for the whole run: the
  // raw `*i64`/`*i32` every property below works through point into them, and
  // only `arena`'s HEADER (`a`) travels into a callee.
  let hbuf = []i64(heapWords)
  let h = ptrOf(hbuf)
  let lbuf = []i32(1)
  let lk = ptrOf(lbuf)
  atomicStore(lk, spinUnlocked)
  heapInit(h)

  let arena = []i64(ARENA_WORDS)
  let abase = int(ptrOf(arena))
  let a = []i64(3)
  a[A_NEXT] = allocAlignUp(abase, allocSpanBytes)
  a[A_LIMIT] = abase + ARENA_WORDS * 8
  a[A_SPANS] = 0

  let initOk = 0
  if (heapLiveBytes(h) == 0 && heapMappedBytes(h) == 0) {
    initOk = 1
  }

  let alignOk = alignProps(h, lk, a)
  let liveAfterAlign = heapLiveBytes(h)
  let reuseOk = reuseProps(h, lk, a)
  let carve = carveProps(a)

  // ---- (j)+(k) per-span occupancy and span reclamation, `./spanaccounting.bit`
  let (occOk, occN) = occRun(a)
  let (recOk, recGot) = recRun(a)

  let largeOk = largeProps(h, lk, a)
  let churn = churnProps(h, lk, a)
  let liveAtEnd = heapLiveBytes(h)
  let mappedAtEnd = heapMappedBytes(h)
  let spansUsed = a[A_SPANS]

  // The same cross-check over the heap every other property ran against, after
  // 20000 rounds of churn across 37 classes rather than one class in isolation.
  spinAcquire(lk)
  let mainBad = heapCheckSpansLocked(h)
  spinRelease(lk)

  // `arena` is reached here so it stays rooted across every collection above.
  let arenaRooted = 0
  if (len(arena) == ARENA_WORDS) {
    arenaRooted = 1
  }

  print("tbl=${tbl.sum}/${tbl.weighted} mono=${mono} pows=${powsOk} cls=${classifyOk} init=${initOk} align=${alignOk}/${liveAfterAlign} reuse=${reuseOk} carve=${carve.ok}/${carve.got} occ=${occOk}/${occN} rec=${recOk}/${recGot} large=${largeOk} churn=${churn.ok} sent=${churn.intact}/${churn.checked} live=${liveAtEnd} mapped=${mappedAtEnd}/${spansUsed} chk=${mainBad} root=${arenaRooted}\n")

  // ---- (l) span cache (#3045), `./spancache.bit` -----------------------------
  spancacheRun()

  // ---- (m) classify fast path (#3100), `./classify.bit` ----------------------
  classifyRun()

  // ---- (n) slot index guards + slot count (#4044), `./slotindex.bit` ---------
  slotIndexRun()
}
